The revelation principle lets us replace an arbitrary communication protocol by a
direct one: players report their private information honestly, and the mediator
recommends actions that obedient players follow. This direct protocol is already
finite, because the original extensive-form game is finite. It is not yet small,
however. A strategy for the mediator may remember every message sent by every
player, and the number of such transcripts can be exponential in the size of the
game tree. This section gives the second reduction in the proof of the
polynomial-time result for communication equilibria #citep(<Zhang22:Polynomial>).

= Reducing the Game to Polynomial Size <sec-reducing-polynomial>

== Why the communication game is large

Let $G$ be a finite, perfect-recall extensive-form game. Write $S$ for its set
of physical histories, including histories at which chance or a player is about
to act, and let $N := |S|$. There are $n$ strategic players and one mediator.
The direct communication game augments a physical history $s in S$ with a
transcript $tau_i$ for each player. A transcript records the reports sent by
Player $i$ and the recommendations received from the mediator. The physical
history says what has happened in the game; the transcripts say what each player
and the mediator have told one another.

These are different objects. Two states can have the same physical history but
different transcripts, because a player may have sent a different report or the
mediator may have sent a different recommendation. If all transcripts are
enumerated independently, a crude upper bound has the form
$ |S| dot |T_1| dot ... dot |T_n| $, where $T_i$ is the set of possible
transcripts for Player $i$. Even when the physical game is modest, a transcript
can contain one message for every earlier information set. The product is the
source of the exponential blowup.

#figure(
  caption: [The unrestricted communication game keeps the physical history and every player transcript. Each additional exchange multiplies the number of states.],
)[
  #image("figures/mechanism_design/full_communication.svg", width: 100%)
]
<fig:unrestricted-communication>

The direct protocol is useful precisely because it gives structure to these
messages. A truthful report identifies the private information that the player
actually has, and an obedient recommendation identifies the action taken at the
corresponding decision point. We now use that structure twice.

== Consistent transcripts and deviation continuations

First consider a unilateral deviation by Player $i$. The communication-game
model assumes that the mediator can keep track of the current physical history
and can compare the information relevant to a report or recommendation with the
history that is actually being played. This is the observability assumption
used by the lecture's construction. It is weaker than giving the mediator power
to force an action: a recommendation remains only a recommendation.

Call a transcript $tau_i$ *consistent* with a physical history $s$ if it is the
transcript generated by the direct protocol along $s$ when Player $i$ reports
the information they have and follows each recommendation. An inconsistent
transcript is therefore evidence that Player $i$ has deviated from the direct
strategy. The mediator does not need an arbitrary, enforceable punishment for
such a deviation. The reduced game simply enters the fixed deviation
continuation specified by the communication-game construction: the mediator
does not reveal additional useful information to the detected deviator, while
the other players continue to use their direct strategies. Any profitable
choice made inside that continuation is still a unilateral deviation and is
represented in the same incentive constraint.

#figure(
  caption: [A recommendation has two relevant continuations: obedience follows the normal protocol, while an inconsistent action enters the fixed deviation continuation.],
)[
  #image("figures/mechanism_design/consistent_transcripts.svg", width: 100%)
]
<fig:consistent-transcripts>

#lemma[Consistent-transcript reduction][
  For every unilateral deviation by Player $i$ from the direct strategy, there
  is a behaviorally equivalent deviation in which every transcript that is
  explicitly represented is consistent with the physical history, except for
  the transcript of Player $i$ after the first detected deviation.
]
#proof[
  Follow the deviating play until the first transcript that is inconsistent
  with the physical history. Before this point, every message is exactly the
  message generated by the direct protocol, so the play is represented by a
  consistent state. At the first inconsistency, the mediator can identify the
  deviating player under the observability assumption above and switch to the
  fixed deviation continuation. From then on, the other players receive the
  same direct information and recommendations as in the original deviation.
  The deviator's future behavior is unrestricted, so replacing the original
  off-path transcript by the continuation transcript does not remove any
  unilateral deviation: it merely records the first point at which the
  deviation has been detected. Thus the deviation has the same distribution of
  terminal histories and the same payoff. Conversely, every path in the
  reduced continuation is a valid unilateral deviation in the original game.
]

The qualification about the continuation matters. The argument does not say
that the mediator may punish a player with an arbitrary payoff, nor that the
mediator can force the other players to take actions. It says only that the
direct communication game supplies a continuation after a transcript becomes
inconsistent, and that all choices available to the deviator in that
continuation remain explicit unilateral deviations.

== Only one deviator matters

The second reduction is an equilibrium observation. For a fixed mediator
strategy $mu$ and direct strategies $x_1^*, ..., x_n^*$, the incentive
constraints are

#align(center)[$
  u_i(mu, x_i^*, x_(-i)^*) >=
  max_(x_i in X_i) u_i(mu, x_i, x_(-i)^*)
  quad (i in [n]).
$]

Only one strategy is varied in each constraint. A branch on which Players $i$
and $j$ both deviate can affect neither the left-hand side nor the right-hand
side of a unilateral constraint: when testing Player $i$, Player $j$ is fixed
to $x_j^*$. Consequently, a state need not remember an arbitrary exceptional
transcript for every player. It needs at most one.

The reduced state therefore has the form

#align(center)[$ (s, s_i, i) $,]

where $s in S$ is the actual physical history, $s_i in S$ is the history that
matches Player $i$'s transcript, and $i in [n] union {bot}$ identifies the
deviator. The symbol $bot$ means that no deviation has been detected. In the
state $(s, bot)$ there is no exceptional transcript to remember. In a state
$(s, s_i, i)$, all players other than $i$ are on the direct path, so their
transcripts are determined by $s$; only Player $i$ may have a transcript that
corresponds to a different physical history.

#lemma[Single-deviator reduction][
  The reduced state representation preserves every unilateral incentive
  constraint of the direct communication equilibrium.
]
#proof[
  Fix a player $i$ and an arbitrary unilateral strategy $x_i$. Players
  $j != i$ use their direct strategies $x_j^*$. By the consistent-transcript
  reduction, before detection all transcripts are consistent with the actual
  history. At detection, the only transcript that may cease to be consistent
  is Player $i$'s transcript. Hence every history of the deviation is encoded
  by $(s, s_i, i)$, or by $(s, bot)$ before detection. The transition rule
  updates $s$ according to the physical action and updates $s_i$ according to
  the information and actions seen by the deviator. The terminal payoff is a
  function of the resulting physical terminal history $s$, so it is unchanged.

  Since $i$ and $x_i$ were arbitrary, the construction represents the best
  response of every player against the direct profile. A path with two or more
  deviators is irrelevant to all these constraints and need not be added.
]

== Counting the reduced game

We can now count the representation rather than merely observing that it has
fewer conceptual cases. There are $N$ no-deviation states, one for each
physical history. For a designated deviator $i$, there are at most $N$ choices
for the actual history $s$ and $N$ choices for the history $s_i$ represented by
the deviator's transcript. Thus

#align(center)[$
  |S_tilde| <= N + n N^2 <= (n + 1) N^2.
$]

This is the promised polynomial bound. It is important that the state stores
the *pair* $(s, s_i)$: keeping only the identity of the deviator would lose the
information needed to determine what that player has observed and which
continuations are available. Conversely, storing one transcript state for every
player would return to the exponential product that we just removed.

Let $A$ be the largest number of physical, report, or recommendation branches
available from one reduced state. Each reduced state has at most $A$ outgoing
transitions, so the number of transitions is at most

#align(center)[$ |E_tilde| <= A (N + n N^2) = O(n N^2 A). $]

For an explicitly represented finite game, $A <= N$ after parallel duplicate
branches are removed, so this is at most $O(n N^3)$ in the coarsest encoding.
More tightly, one can charge transitions to the original tree edges and the
finite report and recommendation alphabets, obtaining a polynomial bound in
the full input size. Every transition updates either the physical history, the
single exceptional transcript state, or the deviator flag; it does not enumerate
any additional transcript product.

#theorem[Polynomial communication-game reduction][
  Under the direct-protocol and observability assumptions above, the
  communication game relevant to unilateral incentive constraints has a
  representation with at most $(n + 1) N^2$ states and
  $O(n N^2 A)$ transitions. It can be constructed in polynomial time in the
  explicit game-tree representation.
]
#proof[
  Enumerate the physical histories and their outgoing branches. Create one
  no-deviation state for each $s in S$. For each $i in [n]$, create the pair
  states $(s, s_i, i)$, omitting unreachable pairs if desired. For each state,
  apply the direct communication rule, the physical transition rule, and the
  fixed deviation continuation rule. The preceding count bounds the number of
  state records and transitions. Each record contains two indices into the
  original tree and a player flag, so it has polynomial encoding length. The
  construction therefore runs in time polynomial in the input size.
]

The reduction preserves exactly the information needed for the next step: the
mediator's objective, the distribution over terminal histories, and every
unilateral deviation payoff. It does not claim that arbitrary off-path threats
are credible, and it does not require a representation of histories in which
several players deviate simultaneously. We have transformed an exponentially
large communication game into a polynomial-size extensive-form game. The next
section uses sequence-form strategies and linear-programming duality to solve
that reduced game and optimize the mediator's objective.

